(function(){
const indiaWorldSources=[
 {name:'Ministry of External Affairs — Annual Reports',url:'https://www.mea.gov.in/annualreports',use:'official annual account of bilateral, regional and multilateral engagement'},
 {name:'MEA bilateral and regional briefs',url:'https://www.mea.gov.in/foreign-relations.htm',use:'country-wise institutional history and official cooperation record'},
 {name:'MEA visits and bilateral/multilateral documents',url:'https://www.mea.gov.in/visits.htm',use:'dated statements, joint documents and current developments'},
 {name:'Parliament questions — Ministry of External Affairs',url:'https://www.mea.gov.in/lok-sabha',use:'official replies, policy detail and implementation evidence'},
 {name:'United Nations Digital Library',url:'https://digitallibrary.un.org/',use:'India’s statements, resolutions, debates and voting records'},
 {name:'UPSC previous question papers',url:'https://www.upsc.gov.in/examinations/previous-question-papers',use:'official demand and recurrence analysis'}
];

PSIR.build({id:'14',title:'Indian Foreign Policy & Non-Alignment',kicker:'Book 14 · Paper II-B foundations',dek:'The determinants, institutions and strategic traditions of Indian foreign policy, including continuity, change and the contemporary inheritance of non-alignment.',prev:'13-united-nations-regionalism-and-global-concerns.html',next:'15-india-and-south-asia.html',objectives:['Explain foreign policy through interacting external and domestic determinants.','Distinguish public doctrine, decision process, instrument and outcome.','Periodise continuity and change from independence to contemporary strategic autonomy.','Evaluate non-alignment as both national strategy and collective diplomacy.'],syllabus:'Indian foreign policy: determinants, institutions, continuity and change; India’s contribution to the Non-Aligned Movement and different phases; current role.',sections:[
{id:'framework',title:'A framework for analysing Indian foreign policy',html:`<p>Foreign policy converts a state’s external environment and domestic priorities into choices. A complete explanation moves through six links: structural setting, national capability, identity and strategic ideas, domestic coalitions, decision institutions, and leadership perception. Geography and power constrain; they do not dictate one policy.</p><p>Separate four objects. A doctrine states general orientation; a decision selects action in a case; instruments include diplomacy, force, trade, aid, technology and culture; outcomes depend on other actors and implementation. Official statements establish the government’s public position. Motive and impact require further evidence.</p><div class="argument-chain">External structure + material capacity + strategic ideas + domestic politics → institutional decision → instrument → negotiated outcome</div>`},
{id:'geography',title:'Geography, security and material capability',html:`<p>India’s continental borders, Indian Ocean location, Himalayan frontiers and proximity to West and Southeast Asia generate simultaneous continental and maritime concerns. Partition produced territorial disputes and a difficult regional security environment. Dependence on seaborne trade and energy adds maritime interests; nuclear weapons and China’s rise affect deterrence and balance.</p><p>Economic scale, military capability, technology, demographic resources and development needs determine available means. Capability is sectoral: market size may create bargaining power while defence import dependence or infrastructure gaps constrain autonomy. As capacity grows, expectations of public-goods provision, crisis response and global rule-making also rise.</p>`},
{id:'ideas',title:'Strategic culture, identity and normative purpose',html:`<p>Anti-colonial nationalism shaped support for sovereign equality, racial justice, decolonisation and independent judgement. Civilisational narratives provide soft-power resources but should not be treated as an unchanging strategic code. Nehru linked autonomy to world order, disarmament and development; later governments retained autonomy while revising partners and instruments.</p><p>Strategic culture is best used as a repertoire of historically available ideas rather than a national personality. Competing traditions include moral internationalism, realism, non-alignment, regional primacy, economic pragmatism and civilisational statecraft. Leaders select among them under specific threats and political projects.</p>`},
{id:'domestic',title:'Domestic determinants and political economy',html:`<p>Regime legitimacy, party ideology, federal interests, business, diaspora, public opinion, media and social movements shape external choices. Economic reform increased the role of trade, investment, technology and energy diplomacy. Border states and coastal regions are affected by migration, connectivity and river agreements, though the Union controls foreign affairs.</p><p>Domestic influence varies by issue. Strategic and nuclear decisions may be executive-centred; trade negotiations engage economic ministries and firms; diaspora crises create public pressure; treaty implementation can need state cooperation or legislation. Two-level-game analysis shows negotiators bargaining internationally while managing domestic ratification and distribution.</p>`},
{id:'institutions',title:'Institutions and the foreign-policy process',html:`<p>The Prime Minister and Cabinet provide political direction; the Cabinet Committee on Security handles major security issues. The Ministry of External Affairs supplies diplomatic expertise and missions; the National Security Council system coordinates strategic assessment; Defence, Finance, Commerce and line ministries control important instruments. Intelligence agencies and armed services shape security options.</p><p>Parliament authorises budgets, debates policy and seeks information, but treaty-making and crisis decisions remain executive-heavy. Courts occasionally affect rights, territory or implementation; states matter in cross-border economic and migration issues. Institutional analysis asks who had information, formal authority, implementation capacity and access to the decision—not just which office existed.</p>`},
{id:'phases',title:'Phases of continuity and change',html:`<table><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Setting and orientation</th><th>Central tension</th></tr><tr><td>1947–62</td><td>Non-alignment, Asian solidarity, decolonisation, development and disarmament.</td><td>Normative activism versus hard-security and capability limits.</td></tr><tr><td>1962–71</td><td>Security reassessment, defence rebuilding, food/economic vulnerability and pragmatic partnerships.</td><td>Autonomy under greater external dependence.</td></tr><tr><td>1971–89</td><td>Regional power assertion, Soviet partnership, nuclear option and NAM leadership.</td><td>Formal non-alignment amid strategic tilt.</td></tr><tr><td>1991–98</td><td>Economic reform, post-Soviet adaptation, Look East and diversified relations.</td><td>Integration without loss of autonomy.</td></tr><tr><td>1998–2014</td><td>Declared nuclear status, US rapprochement, multipartner engagement and economic diplomacy.</td><td>Recognition within unequal regimes.</td></tr><tr><td>2014 onward</td><td>Issue-based coalitions, Indo-Pacific, neighbourhood/connectivity, technology, diaspora and minilateralism.</td><td>Strategic autonomy amid sharper major-power rivalry.</td></tr></table><p>Periodisation is analytical, not a claim that each government began anew. Sovereignty, development, regional security and autonomy persist; partners, capacities, language and willingness to supply regional/global goods change.</p>`},
{id:'instruments',title:'Instruments: from diplomacy to development partnership',html:`<p>Diplomatic instruments include bilateral dialogue, coalitions, international law, summitry and public diplomacy. Economic tools include trade, credit, investment, connectivity and sanctions compliance. Security instruments include deterrence, defence partnerships, exercises, intelligence, evacuation and maritime presence. Development partnerships use grants, lines of credit, capacity-building and technology.</p><p>India often favours demand-driven cooperation and respect for sovereignty, but project delivery, debt sustainability, local participation and measurable outcomes determine credibility. Diaspora networks and cultural ties create influence yet also consular obligations. Instrument choice should match objective, comparative advantage, recipient agency and implementation capacity.</p>`},
{id:'nam-history',title:'India and the Non-Aligned Movement',html:`<p>India helped develop non-alignment through Asian-African solidarity, Bandung principles and the Belgrade summit. It defended decolonisation, anti-apartheid struggle, sovereign equality, peaceful coexistence, disarmament and a fairer economic order. The movement amplified developing-country voice in the UN and supported liberation struggles.</p><p>India’s Soviet friendship and responses to particular conflicts exposed tension between independent judgement and selective alignment. Members differed in regime, region and dependency, limiting collective action. Yet NAM was never designed as a military bloc. Judge its contribution by diplomatic agenda and autonomy, not alliance cohesion.</p>`},
{id:'strategic-autonomy',title:'From non-alignment to strategic autonomy',html:`<p>Strategic autonomy is the capacity to make consequential choices without unacceptable external veto. It does not require equal distance, self-sufficiency or refusal of partnership. India may cooperate deeply with different actors on maritime security, defence, energy, technology or multilateral reform while resisting exclusive alignment.</p><p>“Multi-alignment” describes diversified engagement but can conceal trade-offs. Autonomy has costs: indigenous capability, economic resilience, diplomatic bandwidth and willingness to bear disagreement. Issue-based coalitions can expand options; incompatible commitments can reduce credibility. The test is whether partnerships increase long-run decision space and public capability.</p>`},
{id:'evaluation',title:'How to evaluate a foreign-policy choice',html:`<p>Define the objective and time horizon; identify alternatives; measure material and legal constraints; trace the decision process; assess consistency of ends and means; identify domestic and regional distribution; compare stated and realised outcome; and consider opportunity cost. A summit photograph is an input, not an outcome.</p><p>Balance ethical and strategic claims rather than assuming they are opposites. International legitimacy can be an instrument of durable power; security can be a precondition for ethical action. Equally, moral language can obscure selective interest. A judgement should state which priority was reasonable, which cost was avoidable and what adjustment is required.</p>`}],prompts:[
{q:'Indian foreign policy shows continuity of purpose with change in instruments. Discuss.',demand:'Periodise policy while identifying durable interests and genuine shifts.',thesis:'Autonomy, development and regional security persist, while capability, world order and domestic political economy have diversified partners and tools.',points:['Postcolonial identity and non-alignment.','Security shocks and pragmatic partnerships.','1991 reform and post-Soviet adaptation.','Indo-Pacific, technology, minilateralism and development partnership.'],scholars:'Nehru, K. Subrahmanyam, C. Raja Mohan, Srinath Raghavan.',close:'Continuity is strategic purpose; change lies in how a more capable India pursues it under different constraints.'},
{q:'Geography constrains but does not determine India’s foreign policy. Explain.',demand:'Connect geography to capability, ideas and choice.',thesis:'Continental and maritime location generate enduring security problems, but institutions, technology, economic strategy and leadership determine responses.',points:['Partition, frontiers and regional asymmetry.','Indian Ocean and trade/energy routes.','Capability and infrastructure.','Alternative strategies and partnership choices.'],scholars:'Geopolitical reasoning tempered by foreign-policy analysis.',close:'Geography supplies the board; politics selects the game and develops the pieces.'},
{q:'Foreign-policy decision-making in India remains executive-centred but not socially insulated. Discuss.',demand:'Map institutions and issue-specific domestic influence.',thesis:'Constitutional practice concentrates diplomacy and security in the executive, while markets, states, diaspora, Parliament and public contest increasingly shape preferences and implementation.',points:['Prime Minister, Cabinet, MEA and security institutions.','Parliamentary oversight limits and functions.','Economic ministries, firms and federal interests.','Crisis, media and diaspora pressures.'],scholars:'Two-level games and bureaucratic-politics frameworks.',close:'Influence has pluralised more than formal authority; accountability must catch up with cross-ministerial complexity.'},
{q:'Non-alignment was a strategy of autonomy and an agenda for world-order reform. Examine India’s contribution.',demand:'Cover national and movement dimensions across phases.',thesis:'India used non-alignment to retain choice and mobilise postcolonial demands, though selective positions and member divergence limited coherence.',points:['Bandung/Belgrade and independent judgement.','Decolonisation, disarmament and anti-apartheid.','UN and NIEO coalition.','Soviet tilt and post-Cold War adaptation.'],scholars:'Nehru, Krishna Menon; NAM declarations.',close:'Its most durable contribution is the political claim that weaker states possess agency over both alignment and international rules.'},
{q:'Strategic autonomy is a capacity, not a diplomatic slogan. Comment.',demand:'Define operational conditions and evaluate partnerships.',thesis:'Autonomy exists when diversified relations, domestic capability and resilience allow India to absorb pressure and choose among real alternatives.',points:['Distinguish non-alignment, neutrality and multi-alignment.','Economic, technological and defence capacity.','Issue-based coalitions and partner diversification.','Costs, contradictions and credibility.'],scholars:'Autonomy and hedging literature; Indian strategic debate.',close:'The metric is expanded decision space under crisis, not the number of simultaneous partnerships.'},
{q:'Development partnership is an instrument of influence only when delivery creates mutual ownership. Discuss.',demand:'Assess principles, mechanisms and implementation.',thesis:'Demand-driven projects and capacity-building can generate trust, but delays, weak local participation or unsustainable design convert promise into reputational cost.',points:['Grants, credit, training and technology.','Sovereignty and recipient priorities.','Delivery, procurement and debt.','Local outcomes, transparency and strategic competition.'],scholars:'South–South cooperation and development-effectiveness frameworks.',close:'Partnership becomes power when recipients can use and maintain what has been created.'}],revision:[['Determinant vs decision','A determinant shapes incentives; institutions and leaders convert it into a choice.'],['Doctrine vs outcome','A doctrine guides orientation; success depends on instruments and responses by others.'],['Autonomy vs autarky','Autonomy requires diversified interdependence and capability, not isolation.'],['Moralism vs legitimacy','Normative commitments can create order and influence; moralism begins when trade-offs and selectivity are denied.']],quick:['Six determinants in the framework?','Six phases of Indian foreign policy?','Four instrument families?','Eight steps in policy evaluation?'],sources:indiaWorldSources,recall:['Build a multi-causal explanation of one Indian decision.','Trace autonomy through three historical phases.','Map the decision institutions for a trade or security issue.','Distinguish NAM, neutrality and strategic autonomy.','Evaluate one development partnership by delivery and ownership.']});

PSIR.build({id:'15',title:'India & South Asia',kicker:'Book 15 · Paper II-B neighbourhood',dek:'Regional asymmetry, conflict and cooperation across Pakistan, China, Bangladesh, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Maldives, Bhutan, Myanmar and Afghanistan.',prev:'14-indian-foreign-policy-and-non-alignment.html',next:'16-india-global-south-and-major-powers.html',objectives:['Explain why India’s regional predominance does not automatically produce leadership.','Use a common matrix to analyse every neighbour while preserving country-specific history.','Connect borders, rivers, migration, ethnicity and connectivity to domestic politics on both sides.','Evaluate SAARC, subregionalism, Neighbourhood First and Act East as institutional strategies.'],syllabus:'South Asia; regional cooperation—SAARC performance and future; SAFTA; Look East policy; impediments—river-water disputes, migration, insurgency, ethnic conflicts and border disputes.',sections:[
{id:'region',title:'The South Asian regional-security complex',html:`<p>South Asian states are tied by geography, rivers, markets, languages, migration and security externalities. India is much larger in territory, economy and capability, creating asymmetric interdependence. Neighbours seek access to Indian markets and infrastructure while guarding autonomy and using extra-regional partnerships.</p><p>Regional leadership therefore depends on restraint, predictability, consultation and delivery, not size alone. Domestic instability travels across open or difficult borders; bilateral disputes block regional forums; low connectivity reduces constituencies for cooperation. Analyse each relationship through history, asymmetry, domestic politics, external actors, functional cooperation and crisis management.</p>`},
{id:'pakistan',title:'India–Pakistan: rivalry, deterrence and limited cooperation',html:`<p>Partition, the Kashmir dispute, wars, cross-border terrorism and competing national narratives sustain an enduring rivalry. Nuclear weapons raise the cost of full-scale war but do not remove sub-conventional violence or crisis escalation. The stability–instability paradox, domestic civil–military relations and third-party interests shape behaviour.</p><p>Dialogue has alternated with crisis. Trade, travel, cultural ties, water cooperation under the Indus framework and ceasefire understandings show bounded functionalism, but severe trust deficits and violent spoilers make commitments fragile. Policy choices combine deterrence, counter-terrorism, diplomatic signalling and selective engagement. An answer should distinguish conflict management from conflict resolution.</p>`},
{id:'china',title:'India–China: competition within interdependence',html:`<p>The relationship combines an unsettled boundary, military-security competition, unequal trade, multilateral cooperation and overlapping Asian influence. Agreements designed to manage peace along the Line of Actual Control relied on restraint and confidence-building; border crises exposed the dependence of broader relations on frontier stability.</p><p>China’s economic and infrastructure role in South Asia, maritime activity and partnership with Pakistan affect India’s threat perception. Both cooperate in BRICS, SCO, climate and development forums while contesting regional order and global institutions. A realistic framework separates boundary negotiation, border management, economic de-risking, regional competition and issue-specific multilateral cooperation.</p>`},
{id:'bangladesh',title:'India–Bangladesh: a high-potential partnership',html:`<p>Shared liberation history, a long border, rivers, transit, energy and dense social ties create both opportunity and sensitivity. Land-boundary settlement illustrates negotiated problem-solving; connectivity can integrate India’s northeast with regional markets. Security cooperation and power trade have expanded the functional base.</p><p>Water sharing, border deaths, migration narratives, trade asymmetry and domestic political perceptions remain contentious. River diplomacy must combine basin science, seasonal needs, climate change and federal-state participation. Durable partnership requires broad social and institutional constituencies rather than dependence on any one political alignment.</p>`},
{id:'nepal-bhutan',title:'Nepal and Bhutan: open borders and asymmetric partnership',html:`<p>India and Nepal share an open border, labour movement, cultural ties, security interdependence and hydropower potential. Treaty interpretation, territorial claims, blockade perceptions, constitutional politics and engagement with China periodically generate mistrust. Nepal’s sovereign diversification is not automatically anti-India; Indian policy must combine legitimate security interests with non-intrusive partnership and reliable project delivery.</p><p>India–Bhutan ties rest on close political trust, hydropower, development cooperation and security consultation. Project costs, market arrangements, youth employment and Bhutan’s external balancing require continuous adaptation. Hydropower demonstrates mutual gain but also the need for transparent finance, ecological assessment and domestic ownership.</p>`},
{id:'sri-maldives',title:'Sri Lanka and Maldives: the maritime neighbourhood',html:`<p>Relations with Sri Lanka connect Tamil ethnicity, fisheries, civil-war legacies, trade, ports and Indian Ocean security. India’s experience shows the limits of military intervention without a viable political settlement. Post-conflict reconciliation and devolution remain linked to Sri Lankan sovereignty and India’s domestic Tamil politics. Economic crisis response and connectivity can rebuild trust if sensitive to local agency.</p><p>Maldives is strategically located across sea lanes and depends on tourism, imports and climate resilience. Domestic political competition can frame external partnerships as sovereignty questions. India’s security assistance, development projects and emergency response create value, while transparency and non-partisan engagement reduce backlash. Small-state agency should not be erased by great-power rivalry narratives.</p>`},
{id:'myanmar-afghanistan',title:'Myanmar and Afghanistan: borderlands and continental access',html:`<p>Myanmar links India’s northeast to Southeast Asia and shares insurgency, refugee, trade and connectivity issues. India balances democratic values, humanitarian concern, border security and engagement with authorities capable of affecting projects and insurgent sanctuaries. Instability raises the importance of local communities and coordinated centre–state policy.</p><p>Afghanistan connects India to continental strategy, terrorism concerns and development partnership. India has supported infrastructure, education and humanitarian assistance and historically favoured an Afghan-led, inclusive order. Limited physical access and changing regimes constrain influence. Policy must separate humanitarian engagement, diplomatic contact, recognition and security assurance.</p>`},
{id:'water-migration',title:'Rivers, migration, ethnicity and borders',html:`<p>Transboundary rivers create upstream–downstream asymmetry, seasonal variability, flood and sediment interdependence. A durable agreement needs shared data, basin-wide assessment, minimum flows, benefit sharing, dispute procedures and climate adaptation. State governments and local livelihoods are essential stakeholders.</p><p>Migration may be voluntary, circular, forced or mixed; security frames often obscure labour demand, kinship and refugee protection. Ethnic communities straddle borders, making domestic conflict a foreign-policy issue. Border management must distinguish mobility from violent infiltration, combine technology with legal crossings and avoid treating borderland populations solely as risks.</p>`},
{id:'regionalism',title:'SAARC, SAFTA and subregional cooperation',html:`<p>SAARC created forums and agreements across social and economic fields, but India–Pakistan rivalry, consensus rules, low intra-regional trade and weak implementation constrain it. SAFTA reduced selected tariffs while sensitive lists, non-tariff barriers, logistics and political risk limited integration.</p><p>Subregional arrangements such as BBIN and Bay of Bengal cooperation can advance transport, energy and disaster response without waiting for full regional consensus. They should complement rather than permanently hollow out South Asian institutions. Functional gains can create constituencies, but spillover is not automatic under security rivalry.</p>`},
{id:'act-east',title:'Look East, Act East and the eastern neighbourhood',html:`<p>Look East began with post-1991 economic and diplomatic engagement with Southeast Asia; Act East emphasises strategic, connectivity and implementation dimensions. ASEAN-led institutions, maritime cooperation, trade and supply chains connect the policy to the Indo-Pacific. India’s northeast is a bridge only if border infrastructure, local consent and legal commerce develop.</p><p>Project delays, instability in Myanmar, trade competitiveness and protection concerns limit connectivity. The policy must join physical corridors to customs, standards, digital links, skills and production networks. Strategic signalling without usable economic connection will not transform the eastern neighbourhood.</p>`},
{id:'doctrine',title:'A neighbourhood policy test',html:`<p>For any initiative ask: does it respect sovereign agency; deliver public value on time; account for asymmetric burden; involve affected local and federal actors; avoid partisan dependence; provide crisis response; and leave a dispute-management channel open? Generosity without reliability can disappoint; reciprocity demanded symmetrically from small states can appear coercive.</p><p>Neighbourhood First should be treated as an implementation discipline. Security, connectivity, climate resilience, health and energy grids require regional public goods. India’s leadership is credible when neighbours have a stake in its success and alternatives are managed through competitive quality rather than veto.</p><div class="argument-chain">Asymmetry + shared geography → sensitivity and suspicion → restraint, consultation and reliable delivery → legitimate regional leadership</div>`}],prompts:[
{q:'India’s preponderance in South Asia is a source of both capability and mistrust. Discuss.',demand:'Explain asymmetry and the conditions for legitimate leadership.',thesis:'Size enables public-goods provision and crisis response, but neighbours fear dependence; restraint, consultation and reliable reciprocity convert predominance into leadership.',points:['Asymmetric interdependence.','Domestic nationalism and extra-regional balancing.','Connectivity, markets and crisis assistance.','Rules, delivery and small-state agency.'],scholars:'Regional-security-complex and hegemonic-stability insights.',close:'Leadership is an accepted relationship, not a capability India can declare unilaterally.'},
{q:'Nuclear deterrence has stabilised India–Pakistan war while permitting lower-level conflict. Examine.',demand:'Assess the stability–instability claim and escalation risks.',thesis:'Mutual nuclear vulnerability constrains major war, but sub-conventional violence, short decision time and doctrinal uncertainty create recurrent crisis danger.',points:['Enduring rivalry and territorial dispute.','Deterrence and second-strike logic.','Terrorism, limited-war concepts and signalling.','Dialogue, ceasefire and escalation control.'],scholars:'Glenn Snyder’s paradox; South Asian deterrence literature.',close:'Strategic stability requires conventional restraint, communication and action against violent spoilers, not nuclear possession alone.'},
{q:'India–China relations require compartmentalisation, but the boundary cannot be compartmentalised away. Discuss.',demand:'Balance interdependence and multilateral cooperation against security primacy.',thesis:'Issue-specific engagement remains useful, yet disrupted frontier peace changes threat perception and the political foundation of broader cooperation.',points:['Boundary and LAC management.','Trade asymmetry and technology dependence.','South Asia/Indian Ocean competition.','BRICS/SCO/climate cooperation and limits.'],scholars:'Security dilemma, asymmetric interdependence and complex-interdependence frameworks.',close:'Stable coexistence needs credible border arrangements alongside selective economic and multilateral engagement.'},
{q:'India–Bangladesh relations show the possibilities and limits of functional cooperation in South Asia. Analyse.',demand:'Use achievements and unresolved interdependence.',thesis:'Boundary settlement, security and connectivity demonstrate mutual gain, while rivers and domestic perceptions show why technical cooperation requires political legitimacy.',points:['Land boundary and border management.','Transit, energy and northeast connectivity.','Water, migration and trade concerns.','Broad-based domestic constituencies.'],scholars:'Functionalism and two-level games.',close:'The partnership is most durable when benefits are visible, distributed and institutionally protected across political change.'},
{q:'How should India balance democratic values and security interests in Myanmar?',demand:'Reject a false binary and propose differentiated instruments.',thesis:'Border security and connectivity require engagement, while humanitarian support, political inclusion and non-recognition distinctions preserve normative and long-term interests.',points:['Insurgency, refugees and northeast connectivity.','Authority, access and project protection.','Democratic legitimacy and humanitarian need.','ASEAN coordination, local communities and calibrated contact.'],scholars:'Pragmatic engagement and human-security frameworks.',close:'Principled pragmatism keeps necessary channels open without treating short-term access as endorsement.'},
{q:'Why has SAFTA not produced deep South Asian economic integration?',demand:'Move beyond tariff schedules to political economy and logistics.',thesis:'Sensitive lists, non-tariff barriers and weak connectivity matter, but enduring security rivalry prevents credible expectations and private investment.',points:['SAFTA design and trade structure.','India–Pakistan blockage and consensus.','Logistics, standards and informal trade.','Subregional routes and public goods.'],scholars:'Regional integration, functionalist and political-economy approaches.',close:'Integration requires political risk reduction and trade facilitation as much as formal preference.'},
{q:'Transboundary water should be negotiated as benefit sharing, not only volume sharing. Discuss.',demand:'Explain basin interdependence and institutional design.',thesis:'Joint flood control, power, navigation, ecology and data can enlarge gains beyond a zero-sum allocation of seasonal flow.',points:['Upstream–downstream asymmetry and variability.','Basin data and climate uncertainty.','Local/federal stakeholder inclusion.','Dispute settlement and adaptive review.'],scholars:'Hydro-diplomacy and institutional cooperation frameworks.',close:'A resilient treaty distributes both water and the gains, risks and knowledge of managing a shared basin.'}],revision:[['Regional predominance vs hegemony','Preponderance is material asymmetry; hegemony adds accepted leadership and rule-making.'],['Border vs borderland','A boundary is a legal line; borderlands are lived social and economic regions.'],['Dialogue vs concession','Communication manages information and risk; it does not predetermine substantive compromise.'],['Connectivity vs corridor','Infrastructure becomes connectivity only with rules, services, security and economic use.']],quick:['Six parts of the neighbour matrix?','Four India–China compartments?','Six river-agreement elements?','Four SAARC/SAFTA constraints?'],sources:indiaWorldSources,recall:['Map every neighbour through the common matrix.','Explain asymmetric interdependence.','Design an India–Pakistan crisis-stability answer.','Compare two river disputes institutionally.','Evaluate Act East through physical and non-physical connectivity.']});

PSIR.build({id:'16',title:'India, Global South & Major Powers',kicker:'Book 16 · Paper II-B partnerships',dek:'India’s engagement with Africa, Latin America and the Global South alongside its differentiated relationships with the United States, European Union, Japan, China and Russia.',prev:'15-india-and-south-asia.html',next:'17-nuclear-policy-and-contemporary-foreign-policy-dossier.html',objectives:['Treat the Global South as a political coalition and structural position, not a homogeneous geography.','Evaluate South–South cooperation through agency, delivery and distribution.','Use a common strategic matrix for India’s major-power relations.','Explain simultaneous convergence and divergence without forcing “friend or foe” labels.'],syllabus:'India and the Global South—Africa and Latin America, NIEO and WTO; India and major centres of power—the USA, EU, Japan, China and Russia.',sections:[
{id:'global-south',title:'What is the Global South?',html:`<p>The Global South refers to historically structured disadvantage in colonialism, development finance, technology and rule-making, and to political coalitions seeking reform. It is not simply the Southern Hemisphere or a unified interest group. China, small island states, least-developed economies, commodity exporters and emerging powers occupy different positions.</p><p>Coalition is issue-specific. States may cooperate on climate equity, trade policy space, health technology or institutional reform while competing for markets and status. India’s claim to bridge North and South depends on representing concerns, supplying usable cooperation and accepting scrutiny of its own interests.</p>`},
{id:'south-south',title:'South–South cooperation and development partnership',html:`<p>South–South cooperation stresses solidarity, sovereignty, mutual benefit, capacity-building and demand-driven projects. India uses training, grants, credit, digital public infrastructure, health, disaster relief and technical partnerships. Unlike traditional aid language, it frames recipients as partners; material asymmetry nevertheless remains.</p><p>Evaluation should examine local priority, procurement, debt, employment, ecological effect, maintenance and transparency. Competition with other providers can expand options for recipients, but projects designed mainly for geopolitical visibility reproduce hierarchy. Triangular cooperation can combine resources while risking donor fragmentation.</p>`},
{id:'africa',title:'India and Africa',html:`<p>Relations draw on anti-colonial solidarity, diaspora connections, Indian Ocean geography, trade, energy, minerals, pharmaceuticals, education and peacekeeping. India–Africa Forum Summits created a continent-wide political framework, while bilateral relations reflect diverse African priorities. Capacity-building and affordable health products are important assets.</p><p>Challenges include project delivery, limited diplomatic/commercial presence, local employment concerns, competition among external partners and the danger of treating Africa as a resource arena. African Union membership in the G20 strengthened institutional voice. A mature policy works through African regional organisations and locally defined value addition rather than only commodity access.</p>`},
{id:'latin',title:'India and Latin America and the Caribbean',html:`<p>Distance and weak connectivity long limited relations, but energy, critical minerals, agriculture, pharmaceuticals, IT, space and climate cooperation create complementarity. Brazil is a major partner through BRICS, IBSA and G20; ties across Mexico, Argentina, Chile, the Caribbean and others require country-specific attention.</p><p>Trade is often commodity-concentrated and vulnerable to logistics and price cycles. Language capacity, credit, direct shipping and diplomatic coverage affect expansion. Cooperation on food and energy security must be reconciled with environmental and indigenous rights. Latin America is not merely an extension of Global South forums; its states have distinct regional and great-power strategies.</p>`},
{id:'global-economic',title:'India, NIEO and WTO coalitions',html:`<p>India supported NIEO demands for economic sovereignty, commodity stability, technology and institutional voice. In the WTO it has defended development differentiation, food-security policy space, access to medicines and movement of services while also seeking export opportunity. Coalition partners vary by issue.</p><p>A negotiation answer should identify the rule, Indian interest, affected domestic groups, coalition, counter-position and feasible bargain. “Leadership of the South” requires proposing workable public goods, not only blocking. India’s status as both developing economy and major market produces role tension: it seeks flexibility while other members expect greater contribution.</p>`},
{id:'matrix',title:'The major-power relationship matrix',html:`<p>Analyse every relationship through eight dimensions: order and threat perception; defence/security; trade and investment; technology/critical supply chains; energy; multilateral coordination; diaspora/society; and disputes/constraints. Then identify asymmetry, domestic constituencies and a two-to-five-year trajectory.</p><p>Partnership labels are evidence of diplomatic intent, not analysis. Convergence can be strong in one domain and weak in another. Triangular effects matter: India–US ties affect China and Russia calculations; India–Japan cooperation operates through the Indo-Pacific; EU rules affect Indian firms.</p>`},
{id:'usa',title:'India and the United States',html:`<p>The relationship moved from Cold War estrangement and nuclear disagreement toward broad strategic partnership. Convergence includes Indo-Pacific order, maritime security, defence interoperability, counter-terrorism, technology, education, diaspora and investment. The civil nuclear initiative helped address India’s exceptional position outside the NPT and symbolised political trust.</p><p>Divergence persists over trade, mobility, sanctions exposure, policy toward third countries, human-rights criticism and the meaning of alliance. India seeks advanced technology and balance without treaty alignment; the United States seeks capable partners and greater strategic coordination. The relationship is structurally important but must be evaluated by co-production, regulatory access, supply-chain outcomes and management of disagreement.</p>`},
{id:'eu',title:'India and the European Union',html:`<p>The EU is a major market, investment and technology partner and a regulatory power in trade, data, carbon and sustainability standards. Cooperation covers connectivity, clean energy, maritime security, research and multilateralism. Relations are institutionally dense but can lack the strategic visibility of bilateral ties with major member states.</p><p>Trade negotiations face market access, services, intellectual property, procurement and sustainability questions. EU regulations can function as global standards through market size, creating both compliance costs and opportunities for Indian upgrading. Differences over normative conditionality and external conflicts require sustained political dialogue.</p>`},
{id:'japan',title:'India and Japan',html:`<p>India–Japan relations are driven by maritime order, shared concern over coercive changes, infrastructure, investment, technology and institutional coordination through the Quad and other forums. Japanese finance has supported transport and industrial projects; civil nuclear cooperation marked strategic trust.</p><p>Economic potential is constrained by implementation delays, business environment, language and limited trade integration. Japan’s security evolution and India’s autonomy produce complementary but not identical strategies. Quality infrastructure, supply-chain resilience and joint capacity in third countries can turn political convergence into material effect.</p>`},
{id:'russia',title:'India and Russia',html:`<p>The Soviet relationship supported industrialisation, defence capability, diplomatic backing and strategic autonomy; post-Soviet ties retained defence, nuclear, energy and space cooperation. Russia remains important for military platforms, continental Eurasia and a multipolar diplomatic balance.</p><p>Constraints include Russia’s closer relations with China, its engagement with Pakistan, sanctions and payment/logistics risks, and India’s diversification toward Western and indigenous systems. Energy opportunity and legacy defence dependence create short-term complementarity, while technology access and geopolitical trajectories shape the future. Partnership management requires transition planning, not either nostalgia or abrupt abandonment.</p>`},
{id:'china-link',title:'China as a major power and systemic competitor',html:`<p>Beyond the bilateral boundary, China affects India through Asian polarity, global institutions, manufacturing and technology networks, development finance and maritime presence. Its market and supply-chain role creates economic exposure; its scale shapes India’s defence and partnership choices.</p><p>India responds through internal capacity, selective de-risking, border preparedness, regional partnerships and multilateral bargaining. External balancing cannot substitute for domestic industrial and technological capability. Competition need not foreclose climate, trade or multilateral cooperation where reciprocal interests exist, but stable engagement requires credible deterrence and rules.</p>`},
{id:'strategy',title:'Partnership diversification and issue-based coalitions',html:`<p>India participates in institutions with different memberships—UN, G20, BRICS, SCO, Quad, IBSA and issue coalitions—because no single forum addresses all interests. This “multi-institutional” strategy can bridge orders and prevent exclusive dependence. It can also generate conflicting expectations and limited diplomatic bandwidth.</p><p>Choose coalitions by purpose, added capability, rule compatibility, exit cost and effect on autonomy. Minilateral groups act faster but need wider legitimacy where decisions affect non-members. Bridging succeeds when India articulates compatible bargains; ambiguity alone is not bridge-building.</p><div class="argument-chain">Domestic capability + diversified partnerships + issue coalition → leverage and resilience → autonomy, if contradictions and delivery are managed</div>`}],prompts:[
{q:'The Global South is a coalition to be constructed, not a bloc that already exists. Discuss.',demand:'Explain shared structure, internal diversity and issue-based action.',thesis:'Colonial and economic hierarchy produce common claims, but varied capabilities and interests require active coalition-building around specific rules and public goods.',points:['Historical and structural meaning.','Differences among emerging, least-developed and vulnerable states.','Climate, trade, finance and institutional reform coalitions.','India’s bridge and provider claims.'],scholars:'Dependency, postcolonial and coalition theory.',close:'Political solidarity becomes credible through negotiated positions and delivered cooperation, not geographic rhetoric.'},
{q:'How can India distinguish South–South partnership from a new hierarchy?',demand:'Propose evaluative criteria for development cooperation.',thesis:'Recipient ownership, local value, transparent finance and maintainable outcomes turn asymmetric assistance into partnership.',points:['Demand-driven and capacity-building principles.','Procurement, debt and local employment.','Project delivery and ecological/social impact.','Competition, accountability and recipient alternatives.'],scholars:'Development-effectiveness and postcolonial critiques.',close:'Solidarity must be institutionalised in who decides, who benefits and who can hold the project accountable.'},
{q:'India–US relations rest on convergence without alliance. Examine.',demand:'Cover strategic drivers, instruments and persistent limits.',thesis:'China-related and technological-maritime convergence supports deep cooperation, while India’s autonomy, third-country ties and trade/normative disputes set boundaries.',points:['Post-Cold War and civil-nuclear transformation.','Defence, Indo-Pacific, Quad and technology.','Trade, mobility and diaspora.','Alliance expectations and policy differences.'],scholars:'Balance-of-power, strategic-autonomy and complex-interdependence lenses.',close:'Durability comes from institutionalised capability creation and managed disagreement, not assumed identity of interests.'},
{q:'The European Union exercises power through regulation as much as diplomacy. Discuss implications for India.',demand:'Explain the mechanism and mixed effects of market-based rule diffusion.',thesis:'Access to the EU market transmits data, climate and product standards, imposing adaptation costs while incentivising technological and governance upgrading.',points:['Market size and Brussels effect.','Trade and investment relationship.','Carbon, data and sustainability rules.','Negotiation, domestic capability and strategic partnership.'],scholars:'Regulatory-power Europe and interdependence frameworks.',close:'India should shape standards early and invest in compliance capacity rather than frame every rule as either protectionism or progress.'},
{q:'India–Japan strategic convergence exceeds the depth of their economic integration. Analyse.',demand:'Compare security alignment with trade/investment delivery.',thesis:'Shared Indo-Pacific concerns and infrastructure vision are strong, while firm-level, regulatory and implementation barriers slow economic conversion.',points:['Maritime order and Quad.','Infrastructure, finance and technology.','Trade, supply chains and business barriers.','Third-country cooperation and autonomy.'],scholars:'Balance, institutional and developmental-state approaches.',close:'The relationship’s test is converting political trust into resilient production and technology networks.'},
{q:'India–Russia relations require a strategy of adaptation beyond legacy ties. Discuss.',demand:'Acknowledge enduring value and structural change.',thesis:'Defence, energy and Eurasian interests remain substantial, but sanctions, Russia–China proximity and Indian diversification require managed transition.',points:['Soviet legacy and strategic autonomy.','Defence platforms, nuclear and energy.','Geopolitical and payment/logistics constraints.','Indigenisation and diversified cooperation.'],scholars:'Path dependence and strategic-diversification frameworks.',close:'Adaptation should preserve useful capability while reducing single-source vulnerability and false sentimentality.'},
{q:'India’s participation in both BRICS and the Quad is not inherently contradictory. Comment.',demand:'Explain issue-based institutional strategy and its limits.',thesis:'The forums address different problems and partners, enabling voice in global reform and Indo-Pacific coordination; contradiction arises only where commitments become operationally incompatible.',points:['Different mandates and membership.','Strategic autonomy and forum shopping.','Public goods, legitimacy and minilateral speed.','Credibility, bandwidth and conflicting expectations.'],scholars:'Regime-complex and minilateralism literature.',close:'Multiple memberships create leverage when guided by clear purposes, not when used to avoid choice indefinitely.'}],revision:[['Global South vs developing countries','The former is a political-historical relation and coalition claim; the latter is an economic classification.'],['Partnership vs alignment','Partnership can be domain-specific; alignment implies patterned support against a threat.'],['Diversification vs indecision','Diversification reduces dependence when priorities and red lines remain clear.'],['Trade volume vs strategic value','Volume shows exchange; strategic value includes substitutability, technology, resilience and rules.']],quick:['Eight dimensions of the major-power matrix?','Six development-partnership tests?','Four India–US convergences and divergences?','Five coalition-choice criteria?'],sources:indiaWorldSources,recall:['Define the Global South without geography alone.','Evaluate one India–Africa project category.','Compare US, EU, Japan, Russia and China with the same matrix.','Explain regulatory power with an Indian example.','Judge whether one minilateral forum expands strategic autonomy.']});

PSIR.build({id:'17',title:'Nuclear Policy & Contemporary Foreign-Policy Dossier',kicker:'Book 17 · Paper II-B dynamic issues',dek:'India’s nuclear doctrine, disarmament and UN ambitions, followed by a date-disciplined method for Afghanistan, West Asia, the United States, Israel and emerging strategic issues.',prev:'16-india-global-south-and-major-powers.html',next:'18-pyq-theme-workbook.html',objectives:['Explain the evolution and internal logic of India’s nuclear policy.','Evaluate doctrine through capability, credibility, escalation and political purpose.','Connect India’s UN role and Security Council claim to contribution and reform.','Maintain current-affairs dossiers that distinguish verified development, official position, inference and judgement.'],syllabus:'India and the UN system; demand for a permanent Security Council seat; the nuclear question; changing perceptions and policy; recent developments in Afghanistan, Iraq and West Asia; growing relations with the USA and Israel; vision of a new world order.',sections:[
{id:'nuclear-history',title:'Evolution of India’s nuclear policy',html:`<p>India linked atomic energy to development, scientific autonomy and disarmament while resisting a global order that institutionalised unequal nuclear status. The 1974 peaceful nuclear explosion demonstrated capability without immediate weapon deployment. The 1998 tests declared nuclear-weapon status and were followed by doctrine, command structures and diplomatic efforts to normalise external nuclear cooperation.</p><p>Policy reflects security threats, prestige, technological autonomy, domestic institutions and normative opposition to discriminatory regimes. The civil-nuclear opening with the United States and Nuclear Suppliers Group waiver enabled commerce without NPT accession, creating exceptional integration while India retained its disarmament position.</p>`},
{id:'doctrine',title:'Doctrine: credible minimum deterrence',html:`<p>India’s public doctrine is organised around credible minimum deterrence, civilian political control, survivable retaliation and a declared no-first-use posture with stated qualifications. “Minimum” is dynamic because adversary capability and defence technology change; “credible” requires survivability, communication and command; “deterrence” is political influence, not war-fighting victory.</p><p>A triad and secure command arrangements aim to preserve second strike. Multiple adversaries, missile defence, tactical systems, cyber risk and counterforce debates complicate stability. Doctrinal ambiguity can create flexibility but weaken signalling. Evaluate proposals for change by threat, escalation, force cost, civilian control and diplomatic consequence rather than muscular rhetoric.</p>`},
{id:'disarmament',title:'Non-proliferation and disarmament diplomacy',html:`<p>India has criticised the NPT’s unequal division while supporting non-proliferation, export controls and universal, non-discriminatory disarmament. It has maintained a unilateral testing moratorium and seeks access to technology-control institutions. The Rajiv Gandhi Action Plan remains a major Indian proposal for phased nuclear elimination.</p><p>Disarmament requires verification, sequencing and security transformation; declaratory support without risk reduction is insufficient. Fissile-material controls, test restraint, de-alerting, no-first-use norms and transparency can reduce danger. India must reconcile deterrence needs, regime integration and its claim to normative leadership.</p>`},
{id:'un-india',title:'India in the United Nations',html:`<p>India’s UN engagement spans decolonisation, anti-apartheid diplomacy, peacekeeping, development, climate equity, counter-terrorism and norm-making. Peacekeeping contribution supplies operational credibility, while a large population, economic role and democratic claim support demands for greater voice. Contribution does not create an automatic legal entitlement; reform is a political bargain.</p><p>India’s permanent-seat case should specify representativeness, capability, contribution and legitimacy. Obstacles include rival regional claims, competing reform models, veto politics and the Charter amendment threshold. A serious position addresses responsibilities, contribution to collective action, position on veto and representation of smaller developing states.</p>`},
{id:'afghanistan',title:'Afghanistan dossier',html:`<p><b>Structural problem:</b> Afghanistan’s fragmented political order, geography and external intervention create a persistent security dilemma. <b>Indian interests:</b> terrorism prevention, an inclusive and sovereign Afghanistan, regional connectivity, development gains and protection of Afghan people. <b>Instruments:</b> development projects, education, humanitarian assistance, multilateral diplomacy and calibrated contact.</p><p>Distinguish contact from recognition and humanitarian relief from political endorsement. Pakistan’s geography, Iran and Central Asian access, major-power policies and internal Afghan legitimacy constrain Indian options. An answer should update the governing authorities, humanitarian conditions and official Indian position from dated MEA/UN material before use.</p>`},
{id:'iraq-westasia',title:'Iraq and West Asia dossier',html:`<p>The 2003 Iraq war raised questions of sovereignty, unilateral force, regime change and post-conflict state collapse. India’s interests included legality, regional stability, energy, diaspora security and relations with major powers. The case demonstrates how removing a regime without a legitimate political settlement can produce institutional vacuum and sectarian conflict.</p><p>West Asia policy manages several relationships simultaneously: Gulf states, Iran, Israel, Palestinians and wider Arab publics. Energy security, remittances, trade, investment, maritime routes and the safety of Indian citizens are central. India combines support for a negotiated Palestinian state with expanding ties with Israel and Arab partners. Strategic analysis must not reduce the region to an externally imposed binary.</p>`},
{id:'israel-palestine',title:'Israel, Palestine and India’s differentiated engagement',html:`<p>India historically supported Palestinian self-determination and later established full diplomatic relations with Israel, developing cooperation in defence, agriculture, water, innovation and technology. Relations with Gulf states deepened simultaneously. This illustrates differentiated engagement: pursue bilateral interests while retaining a declared position on Palestinian rights and negotiated peace.</p><p>Conflict intensification creates humanitarian, legal, domestic and strategic pressure. Evaluate India’s response across official statements, votes, aid, evacuation, bilateral diplomacy and long-term principles. Do not infer abandonment or alignment from one vote without reading the resolution text, explanation of vote and pattern.</p>`},
{id:'usa-current',title:'United States dossier: capability partnership and policy friction',html:`<p>The relationship’s current agenda includes defence, critical and emerging technology, resilient supply chains, energy, education, mobility and Indo-Pacific coordination. Institutional mechanisms and private-sector links make it broader than leader chemistry. Technology cooperation should be measured by transfer, co-development, standards access and talent flows.</p><p>Friction can arise over trade, visas, sanctions, third-country relations and normative criticism. Domestic politics in both countries matters. Use the latest MEA bilateral brief, joint statement and parliamentary reply; record what was agreed, deadline, implementing agency and result. An announced initiative is not yet delivered capability.</p>`},
{id:'order-vision',title:'India’s vision of a reformed world order',html:`<p>India advocates multipolarity, reformed multilateralism, sovereign equality, greater Global South voice and rules that preserve development space. It also uses minilateral coalitions where universal bodies are blocked. The vision combines status aspiration with a claim to more representative governance.</p><p>Credibility depends on public-goods contribution: disaster response, peacekeeping, development partnership, health, digital capacity, climate action and maritime security. Multipolarity alone is not justice; several unconstrained powers can intensify insecurity. India’s strongest case is for a plural order constrained by law, representation and practical cooperation.</p>`},
{id:'emerging',title:'Emerging dossiers: Indo-Pacific, technology, climate and connectivity',html:`<p><b>Indo-Pacific:</b> map maritime geography, ASEAN centrality, Quad cooperation, China-related balance and inclusive-order claims. <b>Technology:</b> track semiconductors, AI governance, telecom, cyber security, data and export controls as power and interdependence. <b>Climate:</b> connect equity, energy transition, finance, industrial policy and resilience. <b>Connectivity:</b> assess corridors by financing, route security, standards, local value and delivery.</p><p>These files cross old syllabus boxes. Use Book 11 concepts, Book 12 political economy and Book 16 partnerships. Avoid initiative lists. One theoretically interpreted and accurately dated development is worth more than ten acronyms.</p>`},
{id:'snapshot-2026',title:'Verified evidence snapshot: January–May 2026',html:`<p class="date-stamp">Source review: 30 August 2026</p><table><tr><th>Verified official record</th><th>What it can support</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr><tr><td>MEA records the External Affairs Minister’s United States visit on 2–4 February and a United States Secretary of State visit to India alongside a Quad foreign-ministers’ meeting on 23–26 May 2026.</td><td>Continuing high-level India–US and Quad institutional engagement despite policy frictions.</td><td>Delivery of every announced technology, trade or security initiative.</td></tr><tr><td>MEA records the Prime Minister’s visit to Israel on 25–26 February 2026.</td><td>Political salience and widening high-level engagement in a differentiated West Asia policy.</td><td>That India’s entire Palestine position can be inferred from the visit alone.</td></tr><tr><td>MEA describes the Vice President’s 19–20 April 2026 Sri Lanka visit as the first bilateral visit by an Indian Vice President; the published outcomes included health infrastructure and Eastern Province grant projects.</td><td>Neighbourhood engagement combining political signalling with development delivery.</td><td>Long-term implementation, local reception or resolution of every bilateral issue.</td></tr><tr><td>India chaired a BRICS Foreign Ministers’ Meeting in New Delhi on 15 May 2026 with a session on reform of global governance and the multilateral system.</td><td>India’s use of a plurilateral forum to advance reform and Global South voice.</td><td>Consensus among all members or implementation of a particular reform.</td></tr></table><p>These anchors are examples of evidence classification. Before an examination, replace or supplement them with later primary material and open the underlying outcome documents; do not treat the visit register as substantive proof.</p>`},
{id:'update',title:'The monthly current-affairs update protocol',html:`<ol><li>Choose only syllabus-tagged issues.</li><li>Record the event and exact date from a primary source.</li><li>State what the source proves: commitment, legal act, data or implementation.</li><li>Identify continuity and change in India’s position.</li><li>Apply one IR lens and one counter-lens.</li><li>Add interests, constraints, affected actors and scenarios.</li><li>Write a 35-word judgement and one 10-marker thesis.</li><li>Archive superseded facts; never silently mix dates.</li></ol><p><b>Library currency note:</b> the source routes in this book were reviewed on 30 August 2026. Dynamic facts must still be refreshed at the time of revision from MEA, Parliament, UN and the relevant organisation. The official MEA visit register and annual reports are the starting indexes, not substitutes for the underlying joint document.</p>`},
{id:'evidence',title:'Evidence ledger and uncertainty labels',html:`<table><tr><th>Label</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Safe use</th></tr><tr><td>Verified act</td><td>Treaty, vote, shipment, completed meeting or implemented measure.</td><td>State with source and date.</td></tr><tr><td>Declared intent</td><td>Joint statement, announcement or policy promise.</td><td>Use “announced/committed”; do not claim delivery.</td></tr><tr><td>Official assessment</td><td>Government’s stated interpretation.</td><td>Attribute it; compare with outcome/evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>Analytical inference</td><td>Your explanation of motive or consequence.</td><td>Signal “suggests/indicates” and give mechanism.</td></tr><tr><td>Scenario</td><td>Conditional future possibility.</td><td>State trigger and implication; never present as forecast fact.</td></tr></table>`}],prompts:[
{q:'India’s nuclear doctrine is an attempt to reconcile restraint with credible retaliation. Discuss.',demand:'Explain doctrinal elements, their relationship and current pressures.',thesis:'Minimum forces, no-first-use signalling and survivable second strike seek political deterrence rather than war-fighting, but multiple adversaries and technology strain the balance.',points:['Evolution from option to declared weapon status.','Credibility, minimum and second strike.','Civilian control, communication and ambiguity.','Missile defence, tactical systems, cyber and counterforce debates.'],scholars:'Deterrence theory; official draft doctrine and public statements.',close:'Review should strengthen survivability and crisis stability without casually lowering the nuclear threshold.'},
{q:'India’s opposition to the NPT is compatible with support for non-proliferation. Examine.',demand:'Distinguish critique of regime inequality from opposition to restraint.',thesis:'India rejects discriminatory status allocation while supporting export control, testing restraint and universal disarmament, though possession creates normative tension.',points:['NPT bargain and historical cutoff.','India’s security and equality objections.','Export controls, civil-nuclear integration and moratorium.','Disarmament proposals and credibility.'],scholars:'NPT provisions, Rajiv Gandhi Action Plan, regime theory.',close:'Compatibility depends on demonstrable restraint and contribution to universal risk reduction.'},
{q:'India’s claim to a permanent Security Council seat must be framed as institutional reform, not national entitlement. Discuss.',demand:'Build the case and answer representation/feasibility objections.',thesis:'India strengthens Council representativeness and capacity, but must locate its claim within a model that also improves regional and small-state voice.',points:['Population, capability and UN contribution.','Contemporary legitimacy deficit.','Regional claims, size and veto questions.','Charter amendment and responsibilities.'],scholars:'UN Charter and reform models; legitimacy/effectiveness framework.',close:'The persuasive claim is that India helps produce a more representative and usable Council, not merely that status is overdue.'},
{q:'India’s West Asia policy is an exercise in differentiated engagement. Analyse.',demand:'Explain simultaneous partnerships, interests and pressure points.',thesis:'Energy, diaspora, maritime and technology interests lead India to maintain distinct ties with Gulf states, Iran, Israel and Palestinians without a single regional alignment.',points:['Historical Palestine position and Israel ties.','Gulf economic/diaspora relationship.','Iran, connectivity and strategic autonomy.','Conflict, humanitarian law and evacuation pressure.'],scholars:'Complex interdependence and strategic-autonomy frameworks.',close:'Differentiation remains credible only when principles and humanitarian concerns are consistently articulated alongside bilateral gain.'},
{q:'Contact with a de facto authority is not identical to recognition. Explain with Afghanistan.',demand:'Use legal-diplomatic distinctions to assess policy instruments.',thesis:'Engagement can protect humanitarian and security interests while recognition remains a separate judgement about legitimacy and formal relations.',points:['Indian interests and development record.','Humanitarian access and calibrated contact.','Terrorism, inclusion and rights concerns.','Regional constraints and recognition criteria.'],scholars:'Recognition practice, human-security and realist lenses.',close:'A layered policy preserves influence and assistance without prematurely conferring unconditional legitimacy.'},
{q:'A multipolar world is not necessarily a more just world order. Discuss India’s vision.',demand:'Distinguish distribution of power from legitimate governance.',thesis:'Diffusion can widen agency and restrain hegemony, but justice requires representative institutions, law, public goods and protection of weaker states.',points:['Multipolarity, hegemony and instability.','Reformed multilateralism and Global South voice.','Minilateral action and legitimacy gap.','India’s contribution and consistency.'],scholars:'Realist polarity, English School order and postcolonial reform arguments.',close:'India should advocate constrained pluralism: several centres embedded in more representative and enforceable rules.'},
{q:'How should contemporary developments be used in a PSIR answer?',demand:'Set a source and inference discipline, not a newspaper-list strategy.',thesis:'A current event adds value when dated, classified by evidentiary status and used to test a syllabus concept or policy claim.',points:['Primary-source verification.','Act versus declared intent versus assessment.','Continuity/change and theory link.','Counter-lens, uncertainty and updated judgement.'],scholars:'Foreign-policy analysis and evidence-led reasoning.',close:'Currency without conceptual relevance is trivia; theory without refreshed evidence is sterile.'}],revision:[['Minimum deterrence vs small arsenal','Minimum is threat-relative and dynamic; force numbers alone do not establish credibility.'],['Contact vs recognition','Operational engagement does not necessarily grant formal diplomatic legitimacy.'],['Vote vs policy','One vote responds to particular text; policy requires pattern, explanation and other actions.'],['Current fact vs forecast','A verified event occurred; a scenario is explicitly conditional.']],quick:['Five elements of credible deterrence?','Four bases of India’s UNSC case?','Four West Asia relationship tracks?','Five evidence labels?'],sources:indiaWorldSources,recall:['Trace nuclear policy across four turning points.','Evaluate no-first-use through signalling and stability.','Build India’s UNSC case with objections.','Update one dossier using the eight-step protocol.','Write a verified-act/declared-intent/inference distinction.']});
})();
